 "api_error_status": null,
 "result": "**Mechanism:** werkzeug `LocalProxy` objects backed by a `contextvars.ContextVar`. Each proxy looks up the current value of the context var on every access and forwards attribute access to it.\n\n**Objects** (all in `src/tessel/globals.py`):\n- `_app_var` (line 40) is a `ContextVar[AppContext]` holding the current app context. It is the single source of truth.\n- `app_ctx` (line 41) is a `LocalProxy` for the context itself.\n- `active_app` (line 44) is a `LocalProxy(_app_var, \"app\")`, which resolves to `AppContext.app`.\n- `g` (line 47) is a `LocalProxy(_app_var, \"g\")`, which resolves to `AppContext.g`.\n- `request` (line 57) is a `LocalProxy(_app_var, \"request\")`, which resolves to `AppContext.request`.\n- `session` (line 60) is a `LocalProxy(_app_var, \"session\")`, which resolves to `AppContext.session`.\n\nWhen no context is pushed, the var is unset. The proxies then raise a `RuntimeError` with `_no_app_msg` or `_no_req_msg`, depending on the proxy.\n\nHere the request and session hang off the same app context rather than a separate request context. The legacy `request_ctx` name is only a deprecated `__getattr__` shim (line 65).",
